title: FirstLoopFabianIntro2
tags:
---

The man is still waving, even as you approach him. He stands next to a bench overlooking the void lake, Solster standing behind him.
-> "Hello?"
    Sasha: "Hello?"
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        The man seems relieved to be able to stop waving. He was afraid of making a bad first impression. Perhaps not an unfounded fear.
    << else >>
        The man seems relieved to be able to stop waving.
    << endif >>
    ???: Hello to you too! It's a pleasure to finally meet you Sasha. 
    Fabian: I'm Pastor Fabian Goldhirsch, and I'm here to pick you up from station
    << set $FabianMood += 1 >>
-> "Who are you?"
    Sasha: "Who are you?"
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        The man seems relieved to be able to stop waving. He's quick to answer your question. He's picking his words carefully. He really wants to make a good impression
    << else >>
        The man seems relieved to be able to stop waving. He's quick to answer your question.
    << endif >>
    ???: Oh right, I forgot, you must've never seen me before!
    Fabian: I'm Pastor Fabian Goldhirsch. I came here to pick you up from the station! Sasha, right?
    << set $FabianMood += 1 >>
-> "Stop waving. I can see you."
    Sasha: "Stop waving. I can see you."
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        The man is quick to obey. He seems stunned for a second, his cheery demeanour is a little soured, before he replies to you. 
        He's picking his words carefully. He really wants to make a good impression
    << else >>
        The man is quick to obey. He seems stunned for a second, his cheery demeanour is a little soured, before he replies to you.
    << endif >>
    ???: Right, I can see that you’ve seen me!
    Fabian: I'm Pastor Fabian Goldhirsch. I came here to pick you up from the station! Sasha, right?
-> Ignore him.
    Sasha: (Ignore him)
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        The man continues waving for just a little too long, before gradually stopping. Hesitantly, he speaks up for the first time
        You've rattled him. He's off-center now, physically and mentally. 
    << else >>
        The man continues waving for just a little too long, before gradually stopping. Hesitantly, he speaks up for the first time
    << endif >>
    ???: Uhm... Hi. You are Sasha, right? Sasha Yildiz?
    Fabian: I'm Pastor Fabian Goldhirsch. I came to pick you up from the station.
    << set $FabianMood -= 1 >>

<< if $FabianMood lte -1 >>
The man extends his hand in greeting, hesitantly as well.
<< elif $FabianMood gte 1>>
The man enthusiastically extends his hand in greeting. 
<< else >>
The man extends his hand in greeting. 
<< endif>>
<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
    Solster pastors and other religious authorities usually greet strangers with a hug, as a sign of welcome to the community.
    Is he unfamiliar with this tradition? Or does he think that you'd be more comfortable with this?
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
    Seeing his cloak up-close, it is clearly a hand-me-down, not something he bought himself. 
    It's too big for him, sloppily stitched to fit him, and clearly unfitting for him aesthetically. 
    Something pastel would more his style.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
    His words and actions are pre-planned, as an automaton following its programming. //Is this too modern? Nah, they got computers at the science park
    He must have spent his time waiting for you preparing his whole introduction.
<< endif >>
-> Shake his hand.
    Sasha: (Shake his hand)
    << if $FabianMood <= -1 >>
    His hand trembles slightly. He adds his other hand to the handshake to keep it steady.
    << else >>
    His handshake is enthusiastic too, but more restrained. A pastor's decorum.
    << endif >>
    Fabian: I hope you had good travels! It must have been a long journey to get here.
    Fabian: I know myself how slow the train can feel when you're excited to get to your destination.
    << if $FabianMood lte -1 >>
       He finishes off his sentence with a nervous chuckle.
    << elif $FabianMood gte 1>>
        He finishes off his sentence with a hearty chuckle
    << else >>
        He finishes off his sentence with a bright smile
    << endif >>
-> Continue ignoring him. << if $FabianMood <= -1 >>
    Sasha: (Continue ignoring him)
    He sheepishly puts his hand away after a few uncomfortable seconds
    Fabian: Well, uhm, I hope you had good travels to here
    << set $FabianMood -= 1 >>
-> Ignore him. << if $FabianMood >= 0 >>
    Sasha: (Ignore him.)
    He sheepishly puts his hand away after a few uncomfortable seconds
    Fabian: Well, uhm, I hope you had good travels to here
    << set $FabianMood -= 1 >>

<< jump FabianIntroTree1 >>

===

title: FabianIntroTree1
---
<< declare $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou = false >>
<< declare $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy = false >>

-> “I’m still not entirely sure who you are, or why you’re here.” << if $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou == false >>
    Sasha: “I’m still not entirely sure who you are, or why you’re here.”
    Fabian: Okay okay, so one question at a time. I’m not a native of Solster like you, but I moved here close to 4 years ago, pretty soon after your departure I believe.
    Fabian: I have been the youth pastor of Solster since… Well, about less than a month. 
    Fabian: The previous pastor named me as his successor. I believe you knew him, right?
    He trails off a little, as if he only realised partway through speaking that he was making a mistake.
    Alfonz was one of your dearest friends in Solster when you were young. 
    Besides your brother, he was the one person you were really looking forward to seeing again on your return. One of the only bright points of having to return to Solster.
    The news of his death arrived at your mailbox two weeks ago.
    At least you know for certain now that the letter was not a cruel prank.
    -> “Yes. He was one of my dearest friends. I already heard of his passing.”
        Sasha: “Yes. He was one of my dearest friends. And I already heard of his passing.”
        You add that second part anticipating Fabian’s next question. He already looks rather worried that he may have just broken the news to you in such a poor manner, you can spare him from any more sweating.
        Fabian: Yes, I can imagine the news must have shaken you as much as it did me. 
        Fabian: For the past four years, Alfonz was my closest friend as well. He took me as his understudy, and he taught me everything I needed to know about Solster, its faith, and myself. 
        << if $PsychMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there studying psychology and exploring the open world. 
        << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there going to art school and exploring the open world. 
        << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there studying engineering and exploring the open world. 
        << endif >>
        Fabian: If he were here now, I’m sure he’d be quite proud of how far you’ve come.
        A moment of silence passes.
        And another moment more. You’re not looking at him.
        -> Say something. Anything.
            // I intentionally did not repeat this option here as a "sasha: " thing.
	        You try to open your mouth, but you fail.
	        This isn’t the moment. You can’t be breaking down immediately right as you are back in Solster. Not in front of someone you just met.
	        You wipe your eyes, and tighten your heart. 
	        Sasha: “I want to talk about something else.”
	        Fabian nods. At least he knows when to say nothing at all
            << set $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou = true >>
            << if $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou == false || $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy == false >>
                << jump FabianIntroTree1 >>
            << endif >>
    -> “So it is true. Alfonz is dead?”
        Sasha: “So it is true. Alfonz is dead?”
        He opens his mouth, but pauses and looks away. 
        He takes a few seconds before he responds, looking into your eyes.
        Fabian: I’m afraid so. He passed away four weeks ago. The timing was… unfortunate, Well, I take it our letter arrived at your doorstep in any case.
	    So he is the one who sent you that letter. You didn’t pay attention to who signed it when you read it. 
        Fabian: For the past four years, Alfonz was my closest friend as well. He took me as his understudy, and he taught me everything I needed to know about Solster, its faith, and myself. 
        << if $PsychMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there studying psychology and exploring the open world. 
        << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there going to art school and exploring the open world. 
        << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
            Fabian: All that time, he never stopped talking about you as well. Talking about Sasha, the college student, out there studying engineering and exploring the open world. 
        << endif >>
        Fabian: If he were here now, I’m sure he’d be quite proud of how far you’ve come.
        A moment of silence passes.
        And another moment more. You’re not looking at him.
        -> Say something. Anything.
            // I intentionally did not repeat this option here as a "sasha: " thing.
	        You try to open your mouth, but you fail.
	        This isn’t the moment. You can’t be breaking down immediately right as you are back in Solster. Not in front of someone you just met.
	        You wipe your eyes, and tighten your heart. 
	        Sasha: “I want to talk about something else.”
	        Fabian nods. At least he knows when to say nothing at all
            << set $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou = true >>
            << if $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou == false || $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy == false >>
                << jump FabianIntroTree1 >>
            << endif >>
-> “Where’s Dmitriy? I was expecting he would greet me when I arrived.” << if $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy == false >>
    Sasha: “Where’s Dmitriy? I was expecting he would greet me when I arrived.”
    Fabian mouths a silent ‘ah’, then stammers out a response
    Fabian: Ah yes, he was supposed to come greet you yes. He fully intended to, too!
    Fabian: Unfortunately, his work got in the way. He’s helping set up the festivities for today, and he told me he wouldn’t be able to spare the time to come here if he wanted to finish in time for tonight.
	Did your brother ditch you because he had to work on the parade? 
    When was this decided? You haven't heard anything about this. 
    You were quite sure during your whole travel that he was going to be waiting for you at the station.
    -> “Was there really no way he could come?”
        Sasha: “Was there really no way he could come?”
        Fabian puts his hand on the back of his neck, seemingly unconsciously. You’ve put him on the spot.
        Fabian: I wish I could tell you more than what I’ve said, but those were his exact words.
        Fabian: “You need to go meet Sasha at the station for me. I have to stay working here until late to finish the preparations in time for the Parade.”
        Fabian: That is all he said.
        It does sound like something your brother would say. Especially the fact his request was phrased as a demand instead of a question.
    -> “That’s unfortunate. I was looking forward to seeing him…”
        Sasha: “That’s unfortunate. I was looking forward to seeing him…”
        Fabian gestures patting you on the shoulder, though without actually touching you. He might have figured based off of Dmitriy that you like your personal space too.
        Fabian: It was a real shame, I understand your disappointment completely. But hey, you can see him tonight at the Parade!
        Fabian: You’ll have plenty of time to spend with him now that you’re back in Solster, so don’t let this sour your mood.
        That’s something you’ll have to take his word for.
	In any case, it seems that this man is your sole greeting committee to Solster.
    << set $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy = true >>
    << if $IntroFabianTree1WhoAreYou == false || $IntroFabianTree1WhereDmitriy == false >>
        << jump FabianIntroTree1 >>
    << endif >>

-> “So Alfonz has already been replaced, and my brother didn’t even come to see his estranged sibling return. Wonderful welcome. Can I move on into the town now?”
    Sasha: "So Alfonz has already been replaced, and my brother didn’t even come to see his estranged sibling return."
    Sasha: "Wonderful welcome. Can I move on into the town now?"
    Fabian: I… That’s a bit of a harsh way to put it.
	He’s doing his best to make the best out of what has only been a steady stream of terrible news coming directly at you since you stepped out of the train.
	His best is woefully inadequate. 
-> “I don’t know what kind of reception I was expecting, but I thought I’d see at least a few more people around.”
    Sasha: “I don’t know what kind of reception I was expecting, but I thought I’d see at least a few more people around.”
    Fabian puts his hand on the back of his neck and looks around the square.
    Fabian: There's not a lot of people on this side of town today, true. It is the day of the parade after all.
    Fabian: I think most people are still preparing for it. Or they are still at work. 
    Even Solster's most important holiday isn't a free day for everyone. 
    You never were a fan of Solster's work ethic, looking back.
-> “Thank you for coming to greet me, at least.”
    Sasha: “Thank you for coming to greet me, at least.”
    Fabian: It was no trouble at all. 
    Fabian seems pleased by your faint praise.

Fabian: Would you maybe like to sit down for a bit to continue talking?
He points at the bench he is next to. The void lake extends into the distant horizon before it, the town wrapping around as the void wraps around the town.
Fabian: You must be tired from the journey here. You could rest for a moment while I catch you up on everything new here in Solster.
Solster is not a place known for rapid change, but it has been four years since you’ve been gone. Some catching up to speed might be required.
Besides, it’s not like you’re in a hurry. You’re probably going to have to wait to see your brother anyway.

-> Sit down with him to talk.
    Sasha: (Sit down with him to talk.)
    The metal bench is cold, but dry. The ornate patterns on it are designed more for aesthetics than actual comfort, but at least it comes with a good view.
    This bench must predate the void. You know the train station does in any case. You wonder what the view looked like back then, when this square began to be built.
    The view looks the same as it did when you last left. The void lake is dark, uninviting, yet comforting in a way. 
    Its still waters look like a big hole in the world seen from up here, an island floating on nothingness in the distance.
    The houses surrounding it are dimly visible in the clouded sunlight, with only a few windows shining light. Some people keep their lights on almost day-round in Solster.
    Fabian watches Solster with you, in silence. Occasionally he turns towards you, but he does not speak. He is waiting for you to speak first.
    This is the time for questions, and the time for answers.
    << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>


===

title: FabianIntroTree2
---
<< declare $IntroFabianTree2Alfonz = false >>
<< declare $IntroFabianTree2Dmitriy = false >>
<< declare $IntroFabianTree2VoidLake = false >>
<< declare $IntroFabianTree2Elegy = false >>


-> “How did Alfonz die? I haven’t heard any details” << if $IntroFabianTree2Alfonz == false >>
    Sasha: “How did Alfonz die? I haven’t heard any details”
    Fabian: Taken by the dark. He slept with only an oil lamp at his bedside, and it seems that it got extinguished overnight. 
    Fabian: He was very traditional like that, he did not want an electric light in his room.
    He speaks in a solemn, direct tone. He’s very unlike himself, from how you’ve known him so far. He pays this topic the respect it is due.
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
	    He’s does not sound sad, but he is clearly still mourning. His job requires him to handle grief, so he hides it well. 
        When Alfonz was with him, he could more easily be the man he thought he should be, for the sake of his flock. 
        You can tell that he still feels it even when he is only thinking about him.
    << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
        You can picture it happening on a stage before you. 
        The old man, free of regrets or unfinished business, goes to sleep and lets the light slowly fade away, as does his consciousness, and so does his stay in this world. 
        It is almost too... narrative to be real. What a strange way that fate chose to close Alfonz's final chapter.
        You think he'd have found it the best way to go. He did have a poetic soul.
    << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
        If he slept with an oil lamp every night, he must have known how much oil it needed to last him a full night. 
        The mechanics of the situation are illogical. You can't stop thinking how strange of a blunder it is.
        Unless, of course, he put less oil in on purpose.
        But you are not going to consider that hypothesis.
    << endif >>
    Fabian: He died how he would have always wished he would. Peacefully in his sleep, embraced by the void mother. 
    Fabian: I imagine you haven’t forgotten, but please keep your lamp always fueled and at hand. 
    Fabian: The darkness is capricious, and you’re still quite young to be taken
    -> "I’ll have to get used again to always carrying a lamp. You know that in college I slept in complete darkness?"
        Sasha: "I’ll have to get used again to always carrying a lamp. You know that in college I slept in complete darkness?"
        Fabian: Oh really? Quite daring of you! I remember when I first arrived here, I barely dared to try to sleep for weeks!
        He chuckles lightly. His eyes are lost in reminiscence for a moment. He hasn’t thought much about that era of his life in too long. 
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
            The whole outside world in his palm, he chose to come to this town and join a world that none outside could ever comprehend.
            But in all his time here, what colors of the outside world has he forgotten?
        << endif >>
        Fabian: I did get used to it eventually, after Alfonz gave me a stern talking to for falling asleep during a sermon.
    -> “It must have been just as sudden to you as it was to me, then. Are you adjusting well to being the new youth pastor?”
        Sasha: “It must have been just as sudden to you as it was to me, then. Are you adjusting well to being the new youth pastor?”
        Fabian: Oh, it’s a bit early to say. It’s been only a few weeks, and I didn’t even know I was going to be Alfonz’s successor before then.
        Fabian: I thought it was possible, I was his understudy after all, but when he named me specifically in his will…
        Fabian falls silent. You cannot read anything on his face about what he might be thinking about.
        Fabian: If you don’t mind, I’d prefer leaving this topic for another time.
    << set $IntroFabianTree2Alfonz = true >>
    << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>
-> “How has Dmitriy been doing without me?” << if $IntroFabianTree2Dmitriy == false >>
    Sasha: “How has Dmitriy been doing without me?”
    Fabian: Ah. Yes, he’s been, uhm.
    << if $ArtMajor == true >>
        Fabian looks into the distance, distracted. 
        Pretending to be distracted. He’s not too great of an actor. 
        He takes some time before responding, still staring away from you while he talks.
    << else >>
        Fabian looks into the distance, distracted. 
        He takes some time before responding, still staring away from you while he talks.
    << endif >>
    Fabian: The period after you left was tricky for him for sure. He had to adapt quite a bit to your absence, but I think he’s managed to adjust in the end. 
    Fabian: I didn’t know him personally during that time, so this is just what I heard from Alfonz back then. 
    Fabian: But I can say having talked to him recently after the news of your return arrived, that he has definitely missed you a lot. 
    Fabian: He’ll be really excited to see you again, after all this time.
    He stumbles on his words, then falls silent for a moment, thinking. 
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        What is he contemplating to say or not? It seems that he has an opinion that he does not want to share. 
        He looks at you and continues, returning to his usual cheery tone.
    << else >>
        Then he looks at you and continues, returning to his usual cheery tone.
    << endif >>
    Fabian: Let me know what he says once you talk to him. I look forward to hearing how your reunion will go.
    He finishes off with a warm smile.
    << set $IntroFabianTree2Dmitriy = true >>
    << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>
-> “Void lake still looks the same I see. Don’t think anything ever changes about the void here, does it?” << if $IntroFabianTree2VoidLake == false >>
    Sasha: “Void lake still looks the same I see. Don’t think anything ever changes about the void here, does it?”
    Fabian: Void is always void, indeed. The Mother’s embrace is as dependable as ever
    He says this with a smile. The consistency with which the void remains blanketing the town is perhaps what people love most about it.
    Fabian: There have been some new constructions at the island, though. The ferry system has expanded, so they needed a bigger pier. 
    Fabian: Some scientist from Nauka Square wanted to build a diving bell there too, but I don’t think that got anywhere.
    The void lake always stays the same, but Solster around it does change, if slowly. 
    Maybe give it a few years, or a few decades, and it could become unrecognisable. 
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
        At this pace of development though, you doubt it. 
        It’s as if the faster the world outside develops technologically, the slower Solster moves in response.
    << endif >>
    << set $IntroFabianTree2VoidLake = true >>
    << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>
-> “Is Elegy still in charge of everything here?” << if $IntroFabianTree2Elegy == false >>
    Sasha: “Is Elegy still in charge of everything here?”
    Fabian seems taken aback by your directness, though he recovers his footing easily. He does not seem offended, just surprised.
    Fabian: Well, I wouldn’t say that myself, but I understand what you mean, so… Yes, that’s still the case. 
    Fabian: She leads the faithful as she always has, and there’s been little change in her, as usual. 
    Fabian: I imagine being immortal makes one live life a little slower. But you can still find her all around town, doing her most to help here and aid there. 
    Fabian: She really cares about every person in Solster, big or small.
    And they care about her in response. You recall how her name is spoken in reverence in Solster. 
    Not everyone loves her, but everyone respects her.
    Fabian: Right now she is quite busy with organising the parade, but you might be able to talk to her before it starts.
    Fabian: I think she'll be happy to see that you returned to Solster. I'm sure she heard of it already.
    Somehow you feel that she won't care too strongly about it.
    -> “They’re still doing the parade the same way? I remember not everyone liking the Departure”
        Sasha: “They’re still doing the parade the same way? I remember not everyone liking the Departure”
        Fabian: That is true, yes. But at the end of the day, it is a tradition, and the most sacred of all traditions at that.
        Fabian: Elegy finds it important too. She says it ties the community together, which I very much agree with.
        Death is a peculiar subject in Solster. Here, death is everyone’s shared friend. To death everyone goes, so death brings people together.
        That's how Dmitriy explained it to you as a kid anyways. That is why the most important holiday in Solster is essentially a mass funeral.
        -> “Is Alfonz’s urn going to be at the parade?”
            Sasha: “Is Alfonz’s urn going to be at the parade?”
            Fabian: Yes. I’ll be the one to deposit it. He requested so in his will.
            Fabian is expressionless. However he feels about it does not reflect on his face. 
            At least you didn’t miss Alfonz's Departure. Maybe you’ll get a chance to say goodbye, at least.
        << set $IntroFabianTree2Elegy = true >>
        << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>
    -> “Are you going to go to the parade? Maybe I shouldn’t be keeping you here then.”
        Sasha: “Are you going to go to the parade? Maybe I shouldn’t be keeping you here then.”
        Fabian: I appreciate the concern, but don’t worry. I have the time.
        Fabian: I’ll go pick up Alfonz’s urn and head there later, but for now, I have time to rest and talk.
        -> “Is Alfonz’s urn going to be at the parade?”
            Sasha: “Is Alfonz’s urn going to be at the parade?”
            Fabian: Yes. I’ll be the one to deposit it. He requested so in his will.
            Fabian is expressionless. However he feels about it does not reflect on his face. 
            At least you didn’t miss Alfonz's Departure. Maybe you’ll get a chance to say goodbye, at least.
        << set $IntroFabianTree2Elegy = true >>
        << jump FabianIntroTree2 >>
-> “I think I’m caught up now. Anything else I should know before I head into town?” << if $IntroFabianTree2Alfonz == true && $IntroFabianTree2Dmitriy == true && $IntroFabianTree2VoidLake == true && $IntroFabianTree2Elegy == true >>
    Sasha: “I think I’m caught up now. Anything else I should know before I head into town?”
    Fabian: We covered pretty much everything. Solster hasn’t changed too much in these years to my knowledge.
    Fabian: I might be the biggest new development in town, and you’re already plenty familiar with me now!
    Fabian smiles and gives a light chuckle. You wouldn’t say you’re more than just acquainted now, but at least he is not a stranger anymore.
    Fabian: I’m going to head over to the Parade in a minute. I’ll probably see you later at the parade grounds.
    Fabian: The Parade is at the same place as always, at the docks over there.
    Fabian points directly at the docks, down and to the west of you.
    Fabian: Dmitriy should also be around there too. Maybe you could catch him there and talk to him while he’s at work?
    You’re not sure how much of a multitasker he is, but it's worth a shot.
    Either way, the docks where the parade is held is the best place to go for now.
    You still have to talk to Dmitriy about staying in your old room in any case before the day is over.

-> “Thank you for coming to greet me. I appreciate it.”
    Sasha: “Thank you for coming to greet me. I appreciate it.”
    Fabian gives you a warm smile, fiddling with his hands while responding
    Fabian: Thank you as well. I will admit, I was quite nervous about how you would react, with me being here instead of your brother
    Fabian: Since I am the new youth pastor, you are part of my flock too now, so I really wanted you to feel welcome back into the town
    Fabian: I have to go now, but it was a pleasure to have this conversation with you, Sasha.
    << set $FabianMood += 1 >>
-> “I still wish Dmitriy had come here to see me instead.”
    Sasha: “I still wish Dmitriy had come here to see me instead.”
    Fabian’s face sinks a little, like a dog that has just been scolded.
    Fabian: I’m sorry I couldn’t convince him. My hands were tied.
    Fabian: You can talk about it with him. I won’t take up more of your time now.
    << set $FabianMood -= 1 >>

Fabian stands up and walks away, before turning briefly to say a final thing
Fabian: Oh, I do have something I should mention
Fabian: Some people might be a bit, uh, unprepared for your return. You know how they can be, Solster’s a quite tight-knit community.
Fabian: But as long as you are cordial and kind to others, I’m sure you’ll be fully integrated back into the community in no time.
Fabian: Now, farewell Sasha! I’ll see you at the parade.
And so he leaves. You stand up from the bench. It’s time to go.

===